24449:
Put t = \sin^{-1}x so \cos^{-1}x = \frac{\pi}{2} - t with t \in [-\frac{\pi}{3}, \frac{\pi}{4}]
The sum is t^2 + (\frac{\pi}{2} - t)^2 = 2t^2 - \pi t + \frac{\pi^2}{4}
This parabola opens upwards with vertex at t = \frac{\pi}{4}, an endpoint
So the maximum is at the far endpoint t = -\frac{\pi}{3}
Value \frac{\pi^2}{9} + \frac{25\pi^2}{36} = \frac{29\pi^2}{36}, giving m + n = 29 + 36 = 65 → (65)

24450:
Since ^{15}C_k + ^{15}C_{k+1} = ^{16}C_{k+1}, each factor equals \frac{^{16}C_{k+1}}{^{15}C_k \cdot ^{15}C_{k+1}}
Expanding factorials gives \frac{^{16}C_{k+1}}{^{15}C_k \cdot ^{15}C_{k+1}} = \frac{16}{15 \cdot ^{14}C_k}
The product over k = 0 to 12 is therefore \frac{(16/15)^{13}}{^{14}C_0 \cdots ^{14}C_{12}}
Matching yields \alpha^{13} = (\frac{16}{15})^{13}, so \alpha = \frac{16}{15}
Hence 30\alpha = 30 \times \frac{16}{15} = 32 → (32)
